Occurring at an early, sporadic stage, localized physical and biological control is prioritized:  
① Prune the most severely damaged 1–2 fruits along with their connected branches (including leaves), thereby reducing pest sources;  
② Gently brush the infested fruit surface and adjacent leaf undersides using a soft brush dipped in diluted mineral oil (1% dilution) to physically remove mites;  
③ Release Neoseiulus barkeri (a predatory mite) at a rate of 5–10 individuals per plant onto the undersides of healthy neighboring leaves. This species rapidly locates and preys on immature mites. Avoid blanket spraying across the entire orchard to prevent accidental harm to natural enemies. Reinspect after 7 days—if new spots are fewer than 3, control is effective. This approach is low-cost, residue-free, and ideal for initial intervention in organic or ecological orchards.

It is still timely—this falls within the early intervention window. Procedure:  
① Using a sharp knife, make incisions 0.5 cm beyond the healthy tissue surrounding the lesion, cutting deep into the wood; completely remove all discolored and decayed tissues until light yellow, healthy wood is exposed;  
② Immediately apply Bordeaux paste (1:1:10) or Medipax diluted 100-fold after scraping, ensuring the coverage extends at least 2 cm beyond the scraped area;  
③ Avoid rain exposure for at least 3 days following treatment.  
Note: If the affected area is already dry, cracked, and woody tissue is exposed, expand the area of excision and strengthen subsequent tree recovery by applying additional phosphorus and potassium fertilizers while reducing nitrogen input.

Based on the current condition—no visible mites, no typical leaf stippling or molted exoskeletons, and overall healthy fruit and foliage—the risk level is low, and immediate pesticide application is not recommended. For effective control of citrus red mites, monitoring must be the foundation: inspect 10 randomly selected mature leaves per tree (with particular attention to the leaf undersides) using a 10× handheld magnifier. If the average number of live mites per leaf remains below 3 and natural enemies (e.g., transparent pear-shaped predatory mites) are present, no intervention is required. Non-chemical measures should be prioritized:  
① Ensure adequate irrigation to prevent plant water stress;  
② Eliminate dust sources in the orchard (e.g., by watering unpaved paths);  
③ Protect natural enemies by avoiding broad-spectrum insecticides (such as pyrethroids).  
Pesticide application should only be considered when mite populations on leaf undersides consistently rise to 5–8 mites per leaf, at which point a mineral oil emulsion (e.g., 415 Oil at 1.2%) may be applied to the leaf undersides and base of new shoots.

Chemical control is not currently recommended. The appearance of individual mites serves as an early warning signal; non-chemical measures should be prioritized:  
① Rinse the affected leaf and adjacent new shoots with high-pressure water, which can dislodge over 80% of the mites;  
② Conduct a full-plant inspection, particularly focusing on the leaf undersides and tender shoots. If the total number of mites remains below 5 per 10 leaves, consider releasing Neoseiulus nicrophorus (effective at a predator-prey ratio ≥1:200);  
③ Increase irrigation frequency to elevate field humidity—maintaining relative humidity above 60% can suppress mite reproduction.  
Only when a doubling of mite populations is observed over two consecutive days, or webbing and red spots appear on leaf undersides, should selective acaricides safe to natural enemies—such as spiromesifen or etoxazole—be considered, with emphasis on targeted spraying of leaf undersides.

During the initial stage of disease onset, integrated agricultural and chemical measures should be immediately implemented:  
① Remove infected leaves and burn them outside the field to prevent diseased plant residues from becoming sources of secondary infection;  
② Improve ventilation and light penetration to reduce leaf surface humidity;  
③ Apply a 75% mancozeb solution diluted at 500 times or a 50% carbendazim solution diluted at 500 times, ensuring thorough spraying on the leaf undersides (where pathogens commonly accumulate). Repeat the application every 7 days for 2–3 consecutive treatments. Avoid applying fungicides before rain to prevent wash-off and loss of efficacy.

Yes, this mottled rust-colored discoloration results from the destruction of chlorophyll and oxidation of tannins in epidermal cells of leaves or fruits following piercing-sucking damage by the citrus red mite. Initially appearing as tiny grayish-white speckles, these lesions later coalesce into irregular brown spots. Confirmation methods include:  
① Examine the undersides of adjacent leaves closely under a hand lens—if numerous grayish-white specks (exuviae) or minute active red/orange individuals are visible, the diagnosis is confirmed;  
② Compare young and mature leaves: younger leaves are more susceptible; if young leaves exhibit silvery stippling while older leaves remain green, this supports mite infestation;  
③ Gently rub the affected area—if no powdery residue (distinguishing it from sooty mold) and no exudate (differentiating it from canker disease) are present, the damage is likely due to mechanical and physiological injury caused by mites.

Only two individuals were observed at the very early stage of invasion, still within the monitoring and physical removal window; chemical treatment is not recommended at this stage. Priority should be given to non-chemical measures:  
① Gently brush the infested stem segments with a soft-bristled brush dipped in water, or use a high-pressure fine mist nozzle (pressure ≤ 0.3 MPa) to directionally rinse the undersides of tender shoots—this can effectively remove over 80% of the individuals;  
② Prune off infested young shoots (retaining 2–3 healthy buds), and centrally destroy them to prevent further spread;  
③ Simultaneously inspect nearby plants for new shoots to check for any hidden damage.  

If, upon reinspection within three days, five or more new individuals are detected or chlorotic spots appear on leaves, transition into the early intervention phase is warranted. At this point, applications of either 0.3% azadirachtin emulsifiable concentrate diluted at 600 times or 24% flubendiamide suspension concentrate diluted at 4,000 times may be used via spraying. Focus application on new shoots and leaf undersides, taking care to avoid harming natural enemies.

Immediate implementation of three key interventions is essential:  
① Orchard sanitation: Thoroughly remove fallen diseased fruits and leaves, and centrally destroy them to reduce primary inoculum sources;  
② Microenvironment management: Prune overly dense branches to improve air circulation and light penetration, regulate irrigation to prevent waterlogging in the orchard, and promptly drain excess water after rainfall;  
③ Preventive fungicide application: Spray protective fungicides on healthy fruits and new shoots, such as mancozeb at 600 times dilution combined with pyraclostrobin at 1,500 times dilution, applied every 10–14 days for 2–3 consecutive applications. Avoid spraying during high-temperature periods, and prioritize coverage on fruit stems and sun-exposed surfaces. If disease pressure was severe in previous years, apply a post-harvest spray of lime sulfur at 5°Bé to thoroughly clean the canopy.

During the late ripening stage (mid to late color change phase), conventional chemical acaricides should be avoided. Instead, a combined physical and biological control strategy is recommended:  
① Gently wipe affected fruit surfaces with a soft brush dipped in diluted mineral oil (0.5% narrow-range 415 oil) to remove mites while simultaneously repairing minor epidermal damage;  
② Release Neoseiulus barkeri predatory mites into the middle to lower canopy, at a rate of 50–100 individuals per tree. This species effectively preys on remaining eggs and nymphs without leaving pesticide residues;  
③ During post-harvest sorting, remove rust-scarred fruits, and subject storage facilities to ozone fumigation (5–10 ppm for 2 hours) to prevent secondary infestations during storage.  

All measures comply with green food standards, have a zero Pre-Harvest Interval (PHI), and pose no risk to market safety.

Feasible, the core strategy lies in restoring ecological balance:  
① Immediately cease use of broad-spectrum insecticides to protect existing natural enemies;  
② Strengthen irrigation and orchard humidity management—regularly spray clean water to wash off dust (especially in areas affected by road dust), as dry, dusty conditions significantly promote mite proliferation;  
③ Release commercially reared Neoseiulus barkeri predatory mites during summer and autumn (June–September), at a rate of 5,000–10,000 individuals per acre, applied on the undersides of inner canopy branches;  
④ Combine with periodic low-concentration (0.5%) applications of mineral oil (e.g., 99% UR 415 oil), which physically suppresses mites without harming natural enemies;  
⑤ During winter orchard cleanup, remove rough bark and burn fallen leaves to reduce overwintering egg populations.  

When implemented synergistically, these measures can bring mite infestations below economic threshold levels within 2–3 growing seasons.

The current stage is the initial infection period during flowering. Priority should be given to physical removal combined with localized protection:  
① After morning dew has dried, gently shake the branches daily to dislodge infected flowers and remaining petals; collect and deeply bury or incinerate them;  
② For still healthy flowers and young fruits, use a low-pressure air pump to blow away residual petals (avoiding mechanical injury);  
③ Spray 0.3% copper calcium sulfate (a Bordeaux mixture alternative) or 40% pyrimethanil suspension diluted 1,500-fold onto leaves within 10 cm around symptomatic floral organs, focusing on the pedicel, ovary, and leaf axils. Apply every 5–7 days for two consecutive applications. Avoid spraying during peak pollination hours in full bloom to prevent interference with fruit set.

Summer is an unsuitable period for pruning—high temperatures slow wound healing, and pathogenic spores such as Neoscytalidium are easily disseminated via airborne transmission, posing a high risk of secondary infection through fresh wounds. If pruning is unavoidable, the following measures must be strictly followed:  
① Remove only visibly dead or severely discolored branches, ensuring the cut is made at least 5 cm beyond the affected area;  
② Immediately apply a Trichoderma-based biological protective agent (e.g., Trichoderma paste) or Bordeaux mixture to seal the wound;  
③ Remove all pruned material from the orchard within 24 hours after pruning and incinerate it—never grind and leave debris in place.  

A more reliable approach is to delay pruning until the cool, dry period of autumn (October–November), conducting unified orchard sanitation pruning at that time, combined with winter bark scraping and chemical treatment.

High temperature and drought (>30°C, RH < 60%) are key triggers for outbreaks of *Eutetranychus lewisi*. Immediate intervention is required under these conditions:  
① The primary measure is irrigation to increase humidity—drip or micro-sprinkler irrigation should raise field humidity above 65%, significantly suppressing oviposition and nymphal survival;  
② Apply mineral oil emulsion (e.g., 99% narrow-range oil at 0.5%) locally as a foliar spray to coat stems and leaves, killing active mites through suffocation and physical barrier effects, with minimal impact on predatory mites;  
③ Avoid high-nitrogen fertilizers; instead, supplement with balanced organic liquid fertilizer to enhance plant resistance. Chemical control should only be applied when a population is detected on the leaf undersides (>10 mites per field of view), using a suspension of spiromesifen diluted to 4,000 times, targeting exclusively the undersides of leaves and tender shoots.

From the image, the lesion is determined to be in an active exudative phase:  
① The surface of the gum is smooth and glossy with visible reflectivity, indicating high moisture content and lack of drying;  
② Part of the gum exhibits a thread-like, hanging appearance, suggesting it remains viscous and flowable;  
③ There is no obvious cracking or powdering at the boundary between the gum and the bark, and no black sporodochia are observed (in advanced Neoscytalidium lesions, black powdery conidia would typically be exposed).  

Field verification method: Gently touch the edge of the gum with your finger—if it feels slightly sticky but lacks a hard crust, it confirms fresh exudation. Additionally, inspect other branches on the same plant for similar newly formed gum spots to determine whether the disease is currently spreading.

During summer, avoid directly applying chemical fungicides to infected areas. Under high-temperature and high-humidity conditions, spraying can leave moisture trapped in wounds, promoting fungal proliferation. Moreover, most fungicides exhibit reduced efficacy and increased phytotoxicity risk above 35°C. Recommended measures:  
① Prioritize physical removal—scrape away all diseased tissue and apply a protective paste (e.g., freshly mixed 10:1 blend of raw tung oil and sulfur powder);  
② Spray the entire plant with a solution of 0.3% potassium dihydrogen phosphate and 0.2% borax to enhance stress resistance;  
③ Avoid heavy pruning or nitrogen fertilization from July to August. Postpone the use of systemic fungicides such as azoxystrobin or difenoconazole for root drenching or trunk application until autumn (after September), when cooler temperatures reduce the risk of damage and help suppress latent pathogen sources.

Currently in the spreading stage, immediate implementation of a three-step approach is required:  
① Debridement — using a sharp instrument, make a circular cut 0.5 cm outside the lesion margin, penetrating deeply into the wood, and completely remove all discolored bark and gum tissue until fresh, light yellow wood is exposed;  
② Disinfection — immediately apply Bordeaux paste (1:1:10) or Nemex 100 (100-fold dilution) to fully cover the entire debrided area, extending slightly beyond the healthy tissue boundary;  
③ Protection — avoid performing operations during the high-temperature, high-humidity period from July to August; protect the treated area from rain for the first 3 days post-treatment. Additionally, clear fallen leaves and dropped fruits from the orchard to reduce secondary infection sources. Never rely solely on chemical spraying without debridement, as pathogens beneath the gum will continue to spread.

There is still time, and the current period represents the optimal intervention window. The appearance of a single adult mite indicates that a stable population has not yet formed, making the "spot treatment + prevention of spread" strategy most effective at this stage:  
① Immediately rinse infested leaves and the adjacent 3–5 new leaves with high-pressure water (focus on the leaf undersides), which can physically remove over 80% of newly hatched nymphs;  
② Apply a localized spray of 0.3% matrine aqueous solution or 24% spiromesifen suspension diluted to 2,000 times, targeting only the affected branches and the two adjacent leaf tiers, avoiding harm to natural enemies;  
③ Simultaneously clear fallen leaves and weeds from the ground to reduce overwintering pest populations.  
Note: Avoid broad-spectrum organophosphate pesticides (e.g., dimethoate) to prevent killing predatory natural enemies such as Neoseiulus nivalis, which could otherwise lead to subsequent pest resurgence.

Proper use of mineral oil does not worsen existing black spots but instead prevents further damage. The discolored fruit peel consists of dead tissue without physiological activity; thus, the oil acts only on living mites and eggs on the surface. Narrow-range oils (e.g., Type 415 with 99% unsulfonated residue) are safe for mature fruits when applied at 25–30°C under low light conditions. Key application guidelines:  
① Apply in early morning or late afternoon, avoiding temperatures above 35°C;  
② Maintain a concentration of 1.2%, diluted at 200–300 L water per acre, ensuring fine, uniform droplets without runoff;  
③ Focus spraying on leaf undersides and concave areas near the fruit shoulders, avoiding direct spraying onto the central fruit surface to reduce the risk of phytotoxicity. If previous oil injury has occurred in the orchard, conduct a preliminary test spray on a small area and observe for 24 hours before full application.  
Supplement: The oil also effectively removes molted exoskeletons accumulated on the wax layer, enhancing the efficacy of subsequent biological control measures.

Non-chemical measures should be prioritized:  
① Manual removal—use a soft-bristled brush dipped in water to gently brush the dense infestation areas (especially along the main veins), effectively removing over 70% of young nymphs;  
② Biological control—release natural enemies such as Vedalia wasps or ladybugs (e.g., the Australian ladybird beetle *Rodolia cardinalis*) if present around orchards or green spaces. Release adult or larval stages above pest colonies on sunny mornings; these predators can naturally suppress pest populations within 4–6 weeks;  
③ Apply mineral oil (0.5%–1% narrow-spectrum oil) to foliage, which suffocates mobile nymphs by coating their bodies.  

Note: This is currently the peak period for young nymphs. If chemical control is necessary, use thiamethoxam (240–960 g a.i./hm²) sprayed on the leaf undersides. However, avoid broad-spectrum insecticides (e.g., pyrethroids) to prevent harm to beneficial natural enemies.

The affected area must be scraped before applying the treatment. Procedure:  
① Using a sharp knife, make a circular cut 0.5 cm beyond the healthy tissue surrounding the lesion, cutting deep into the wood. Thoroughly remove all discolored bark and gum, exposing light yellowish-white healthy wood;  
② Disinfect the wound using 75% alcohol or a 1% copper sulfate solution;  
③ Immediately apply Bordeaux paste (1:1:10) or 100-fold diluted Mingbingwei solution, covering the entire scraped area with a slight extension beyond the healthy tissue;  
④ Avoid performing this procedure during the high-temperature, high-humidity period of July–August to prevent secondary infection. Chemical treatments should be used only for localized application and must not be sprayed over the entire plant.

The "Clear Source + Isolate + Protect" three-step protocol should be implemented immediately:  
① Remove all dead branches, making cuts at least 5 cm below healthy tissue; completely remove diseased branches from the orchard and incinerate them to prevent spore dissemination;  
② Scrape away all soft, necrotic bark at the gummosis lesion site until light yellow, healthy wood is exposed;  
③ Immediately apply a sealing protective agent—either Bordeaux paste (1:1:10) or a mixture of raw tung oil and sulfur powder (10:1)—to freshly exposed wounds.  
Avoid performing these operations during the high-temperature period of July–August to prevent exacerbating tree stress.

Do not mix indiscriminately. This mite belongs to the class Arachnida and differs significantly in physiological structure from insects (such as aphids), rendering conventional insecticides (e.g., imidacloprid, acrinathrin) ineffective against it. Although some organophosphate pesticides (e.g., omethoate) exhibit partial control effects, they easily induce resistance and harm natural enemies. Recommended measures:  
① In early infestation stages (single or few mites), apply 0.3% matrine aqueous solution by spraying the leaf undersides—safe and highly effective;  
② If chemical control is necessary, use a specific miticide such as 24% spiromesifen suspension concentrate diluted at 4,000 times, targeting only the undersides of leaves and tender shoots;  
③ Avoid mixing with emulsifiable concentrate (EC) formulations (risk of phytotoxicity), and strictly prohibit combining with sulfur-based products within less than 30 days. Always conduct a preliminary small-scale test spray first; proceed with full-field application only after confirming no phytotoxicity.

Chemical control is not currently necessary. During the monitoring and early warning stage, when single adult mites are detected, non-chemical measures should be prioritized:  
① Rinse leaf surfaces (especially the undersides) with high-pressure water to dislodge mites, thereby reducing initial infestation levels;  
② Examine the adjacent five leaves and new shoots to determine whether the infestation is isolated;  
③ If only 1–2 mites remain upon reinspection within three days, increase field surveillance frequency and protect natural enemies (e.g., *Amblyseius nicaeensis*).  
Chemical application should only be considered when there are ≥2 mites per square centimeter on the leaf underside or when obvious chlorotic spots appear. At that point, selective acaricides safe for predatory mites—such as spirodiclofen or etoxazole—are recommended, with targeted applications focused on the leaf undersides.

Blackened fruit peel damage is irreversible, but timely control can prevent further fruit injury and protect foliage. Currently in the spread phase (some fruits already affected, most still intact), immediate action is recommended:  
① Prioritize application of a mineral oil emulsion (e.g., 415 Narrow-Range Oil at 1.2% concentration) via full-plant spray, with special emphasis on the undersides of leaves, fruit stem cavities, and new shoots. This suffocates and physically blocks active mites;  
② If broad-spectrum insecticides have previously been used in the orchard leading to reduced natural enemy populations, consider simultaneously releasing Neoseiulus barkeri predatory mites at a rate of 50–100 per tree, followed by reinspection 3–5 days later;  
③ Avoid using broad-spectrum pesticides such as imidacloprid or beta-cypermethrin, which may further eliminate beneficial natural enemies.  
Note: Oil-based formulations are safest when applied at temperatures below 30°C and in the absence of strong sunlight, to minimize the risk of phytotoxicity on orange-colored fruit.

Currently in the initial scattered outbreak stage, non-chemical measures are prioritized:  
① Immediately remove and destroy infected fruits to reduce pest sources;  
② Thoroughly rinse the entire plant with high-pressure water (focusing on the leaf undersides and fruit stem cavities) to dislodge residual mites and molts;  
③ If active mites (red dots moving on leaf undersides) are detected during a follow-up inspection within 3 days, apply a localized spray of mineral oil emulsion (e.g., 99% narrow-range oil Type 415 at 1.2% concentration) only to affected branches—avoiding whole-orchard spraying to prevent harm to natural enemies.  
Chemical pesticides are not currently recommended, as damage to individual fruits does not reach economic threshold (typically ≥8 female mites per leaf is required before considering treatment), and indiscriminate chemical use risks inducing resistance and eliminating beneficial predators such as *Neoseiulus barkeri*.

The disease is currently in an active spreading phase, necessitating immediate implementation of the following three measures:  
① Prioritize improved water management—apply shallow trench irrigation around the tree base (avoiding flood irrigation) to alleviate drought stress, as water deficiency accelerates pathogen spread;  
② Clear areas with gum exudation: use disinfected tools to scrape away the gummy substance and the underlying discolored, necrotic bark (scraping down to healthy xylem tissue). After drying the wounds, apply a bio-protection agent containing Trichoderma (e.g., trichoderma paste) to prevent re-infection;  
③ Strictly prohibit pruning or mechanical operations to avoid creating new wounds.  
Note: Do not apply fungicides at this stage, as spray solutions cannot effectively penetrate into the infection site, and may instead stimulate increased gum secretion.

Immediate "debridement and protection" measures should be implemented:  
① Using a sharp instrument, perform a circular excision 0.5 cm beyond the healthy tissue margin at the affected site, thoroughly removing all discolored, softened bark and gum material until light yellow, healthy wood is exposed;  
② Immediately apply Bordeaux paste (1:1:10) or Meibingwei diluted 100-fold to cover the entire wound surface after scraping;  
③ Concurrently strengthen tree vigor management—avoid applying nitrogen fertilizer from July to August, prohibit use of uncomposted organic manure, and maintain moderate soil moisture.  
This phase represents an early intervention window; prompt treatment can effectively halt further advancement of the pathogen into the woody tissues.

The tree is currently in an active phase of sap exudation. Immediate implementation of the three-step approach is recommended:  
① Gently scrape the affected area—using a sterilized knife, make a circular cut 0.5 cm beyond the healthy tissue margin, removing discolored and softened bark until light yellowish-white healthy wood is exposed (avoid damaging the cambium layer);  
② Apply protective treatment—immediately apply Bordeaux paste (1:1:10) or Mibinwei fungicide diluted 100-fold after scraping, ensuring coverage extends 2 cm beyond the scraped area;  
③ Strengthen tree vigor—simultaneously increase application of phosphorus and potassium fertilizers, avoid nitrogen fertilizer applications during July–August, and ensure adequate soil drainage.  
Note: All removed diseased tissues must be taken away from the orchard and destroyed to prevent spore dissemination.

Lime water alone is not recommended. Although its alkalinity can inhibit certain pathogens, it cannot penetrate existing gummy layers or eliminate internal infections. Furthermore, it shows limited efficacy against Phytophthora species and may hinder wound drying and healing. The correct procedure is as follows:  
① First, completely remove all gum exudates and the discolored, softened diseased bark beneath, exposing healthy tissue with a light yellow-green hue (retain a 1 cm margin of healthy bark at the edges);  
② Allow the exposed wound to air-dry naturally for 1–2 days;  
③ Then apply a copper-based fungicide (e.g., 80% Bordeaux mixture diluted into a paste) or a phosphonate fungicide (e.g., 50% metalaxyl diluted 100-fold), ensuring thorough coverage of the treated area and extending 5 cm below into the adjacent healthy bark;  
④ Simultaneously clear fallen leaves from around the tree base and elevate the trunk collar to prevent water accumulation.  
If gum exudates extensively encircle the base, assess whether the girdling exceeds 50%. If so, replacement of the tree is advised.

Based on the image features, the damage is more consistent with citrus red mite (Panonychus citri) rather than citrus rust mite (Phyllocoptruta oleivora). Key distinguishing characteristics are as follows:  
① Damage caused by rust mites results in uniformly dark brown to black, "sooty" appearance of the fruit surface, with an extremely rough texture resembling sandpaper, often accompanied by severe leaf curling;  
② Damage from full-grown spider mites appears as irregular patches of gray-brown to dark brown, with a coarser granular texture but not entirely black, typically forming irregular cloud-like patterns, and the fruit shape remains largely unchanged;  
③ Rust mites primarily attack young fruits and rarely infest mature ones—however, the fruit in this image is already nearing maturity;  
④ This image does not exhibit the typical "black-skinned fruit" symptom associated with rust mite infestation. Therefore, rust mite can be ruled out, supporting a diagnosis of citrus red mite.

Relying solely on chemical spraying is insufficient for effective control: The sclerotia of Armillaria spp. can extend more than 1 meter deep into the soil, and dense mycelial mats have already formed at the root collar. Surface application cannot penetrate to the infection center. Chemical agents such as metalaxyl and pyraclostrobin must be applied via root drench (5–10 L per plant) directly to the rhizosphere and used only during early disease stages to be effective. The current image reveals extensive black rot and structural collapse of the root system, indicating mid-to-late stage infection. At this point, root drenching can only delay infection spread to adjacent healthy roots but cannot reverse main root necrosis. A more feasible strategy includes:  
① Digging a circular trench 1.5 meters outside the canopy drip line, then incorporating bio-organic fertilizer containing *Paecilomyces lilacinus* (20 kg per tree) to establish a microbial barrier;  
② Simultaneously pruning approximately one-third of the canopy to reduce transpiration demand;  
③ Closely monitoring neighboring trees for new symptoms—such as yellowing shoots or premature leaf drop—within the next 30 days to determine whether to expand the removal zone.

This uneven distribution results from the combined effects of mite aggregation and differences in fruit developmental stages:  
① Two-spotted spider mites prefer to inhabit inner branches and fruits with poor ventilation and low light exposure, resulting in lighter infestation on outer fruits;  
② Fruits are most susceptible during the period of fruit enlargement to color change. Early-maturing or shade-exposed fruits suffer heavier damage with larger affected areas, whereas later-hanging or sun-exposed fruits experience milder infestation with smaller or merely faint brownish spots;  
③ Mites reproduce rapidly (one generation every 12 days under high temperatures), and localized outbreaks can lead to concentrated damage on individual fruits. Additionally, prior application of broad-spectrum insecticides (e.g., pyrethroids) in orchards may eliminate natural enemies such as *Neoseiulus barkeri*, allowing mite populations to proliferate uncontrollably on specific branches and causing patchy damage. It is recommended to inspect the undersides of leaves on the same branch for active mites or eggs to confirm infestation.

Based on the image, the condition is more consistent with fungal gummosis. Key distinguishing features are as follows:  
① Bacterial gummosis typically presents as milky white to pale yellow, thin and highly fluid exudates without a vinasse-like odor, often accompanied by leaf spotting or ulcers;  
② In this case, the gum droplets are amber-colored, viscous and semi-solid, aggregating into distinct droplets adhering to the bark surface. The affected bark appears dark brown and rough—characteristic of typical fungal gummosis (e.g., caused by Phytophthora or Phomopsis species);  
③ Fungal gummosis frequently progresses to discoloration of the xylem (gray-brown) and formation of visible mycelial strands, whereas bacterial infections rarely invade the xylem.  
For on-site confirmation, cut open the tissue beneath the gum and assess the odor—should a distinct fermented wine-like smell be present, it strongly supports a diagnosis of fungal origin.

The solitary mites appear on the upper leaf surface and may be in one of the following stages:  
① Initial colonization phase—overwintering females, reactivated in spring, first crawl onto new leaves to feed and establish position, but have not yet laid eggs or aggregated;  
② Male dispersal behavior—male *Eutetranychus* spp. frequently actively move to locate females for mating, exhibiting broad mobility without web construction;  
③ Environmental stress-induced dispersion—high temperature and low humidity (<60% RH) drive mites toward well-lit areas on the leaf surface, though aggregation has not yet occurred.  

Field surveys are essential for differentiation: if egg masses (oval, semi-transparent, scattered) or young mite groups are observed concurrently on the lower leaf surface, this indicates early-stage dispersal. In contrast, if only isolated single individuals are found throughout the orchard, it likely represents sporadic invasions.

Do not directly apply lime wash—its strong alkalinity can irritate wounds and exacerbate tissue necrosis. The correct procedure is as follows:  
① Thoroughly remove all diseased tissues down to healthy xylem (stop when fresh, light yellow wood with no exudate is exposed), and smooth the edges into an elliptical shape;  
② Disinfect the wound surface using 75% alcohol or a brief flame sterilization;  
③ Immediately apply a protective agent such as Bordeaux paste (1:1:10), Miebingwei diluted 100-fold, or a freshly mixed blend of raw tung oil and sulfur powder (10:1);  
④ All removed diseased tissues must be taken away from the orchard and incinerated.  
Note: Avoid using copper-containing pesticides during the high-temperature, high-humidity period from July to August to prevent phytotoxicity.

Although live mites were not observed in the images, three lines of evidence allow for indirect inference:  
① The spots exhibit irregular shapes without concentric rings or distinct symptoms (such as mold layers, pustules, or necrotic holes), ruling out fungal diseases like anthracnose and scab;  
② The lesions are concentrated on the shaded sides and lower-middle portions of the fruit, consistent with mites' photophobic and hygrophilic behavior;  
③ Necrosis along leaf margins and faint silvery-gray patches visible on the leaf surface (requiring magnification for confirmation) reflect the characteristic "synchronous injury to both fruit and foliage" pattern associated with mite infestation.  
Definitive diagnosis requires collecting samples from the fruit peel or underside of leaves using adhesive tape and examining them under a microscope for oval, reddish-brown mites measuring 0.2–0.4 mm in size. If only hyphae or spores are observed, the issue is likely a fungal disease. Current evidence strongly supports a mite infestation with a probability exceeding 85%.

There is no need to apply pesticides immediately. Isolated individuals occurring sporadically fall within the natural fluctuation range of population dynamics. Healthy citrus trees can tolerate low mite densities (e.g., ≤8 adult female mites per leaf), and low-density mite populations actually serve as a food source for natural enemies (such as Neoseiulus baraki), promoting ecological regulation. Recommendations:  
① Expand the survey scope; randomly inspect 20 leaves (both new and old) on the underside, and count the total number of live mites;  
② If the average is less than 3 mites per leaf and natural enemies (e.g., Typhlodromus caudatus, larvae of Aiolomorphus sp.) are observed, only strengthen water and fertilizer management and avoid broad-spectrum pesticide applications;  
③ Consider applying a mineral oil emulsion (1.2% narrow-range oil) by spraying the leaf undersides only when two consecutive monitoring periods exceed the economic threshold (e.g., >8 mites per leaf within four weeks after flowering), and always avoid high-temperature periods to prevent phytotoxicity.

Spraying chemical fungicides via aerosol is not recommended during summer for controlling branch and trunk gummosis: high temperatures and humidity accelerate spray evaporation or cause tissue scorching, while most fungicides fail to penetrate the viscous gum layer to reach the pathogenic site. More critically, July–August represents a peak period for disease induction due to alternating high temperatures and drought conditions, during which spray applications yield limited efficacy. Priority should be given to physical and biological control measures:  
① After thoroughly removing diseased tissues, apply a mixture of raw tung oil and sulfur powder (10:1) to seal the wound;  
② Inoculate the treated wound with Trichoderma-based biopesticides (e.g., trichoderma formulations) to suppress spore germination;  
③ Enhance irrigation to prevent drought stress and avoid exacerbation of bark cracking caused by water deficit. Re-evaluate the need for systemic protective fungicide application only after autumn cooling sets in (from September onward).
